//? Title: Voronoi cells: actual nearest-site half-plane clipping
//? Subtitle: Actual native state and retained original experiments
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A finite set of sites partitions a plane by nearest distance, but outer cells
extend indefinitely. Clipping the true cells to a declared viewport gives
complete polygons that can be plotted and checked.
)

\toc

> The method

For site i to be no farther than j, ||x−p_i||²≤||x−p_j||². Cancelling x dot x
gives2(p_j−p_i) dot x≤||p_j||²−||p_i||², a closed half-plane. Intersecting all
such half-planes with the viewport proves every retained point is nearest to i,
with shared boundaries as ties. Each cell is convex because intersections of
convex sets are convex. The algorithm retains actual polygons after each
competitor clip. The older Delaunay-dual API returns only finite fragments of
outer cells; closing those fragments would invent false boundaries.

> Simple worked example

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/voronoi_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = dm::geometry::clipped_voronoi({{2, 5}, {8, 5}}, {0, 10, 0, 10}, 2);
    std::print("| site | competitor | actual polygon after clip |\n|---:|---:|---|\n");
    for (auto q : r.steps) {
        std::print("|{}|{}|", q.site, q.competitor);
        for (auto p : q.polygon)
            std::print("({},{}) ", p.x, p.y);
        std::print("|\n");
    }
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Sites(2,5) and(8,5)");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual half-planes", r.halfplanes));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0}, std::format("Actual shared boundary x{}", std::max_element(r.cells[0].begin(), r.cells[0].end(), [](auto a, auto b) {
        return a.x < b.x;
    })->x));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/voronoi_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| site | competitor | actual polygon after clip |
|---:|---:|---|
|0|1|(0,0) (5,0) (5,10) (0,10) |
|1|0|(5,0) (10,0) (10,10) (5,10) |
\image(figures/voronoi_tiny.svg)
// result_end
